Keep the order of a conditionally convergent account explicit (@math-series-order: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "Ask-whether-one-approximation-bound-covers-the-whole-input-domain.lingua#r_000ADDED5D8664575C3F4E2B6B") { r_002B005B310DF8E3F22C2466D7
If Σ|a_n| converges, Σa_n converges: for any m>n, |Σ_(k=n+1 to m)a_k|≤Σ_(k=n+1 to m)|a_k|, so its partial sums are Cauchy. This is absolute convergence. A convergent series whose absolute series diverges is conditionally convergent. The distinction governs which reorganizations preserve its sum.

For a decreasing nonnegative sequence b_n→0 indexed from 1, consider Σ(−1)^(n+1)b_n. The even partial sums increase because each next pair is nonnegative; the odd partial sums decrease. Every even partial sum is below every later odd one, and their neighboring difference tends to zero. Completeness and squeezing give a common limit. The remainder after N terms has magnitude at most b_(N+1), with the sign of the next term. Eventual decrease suffices after a finite prefix; a merely alternating sign pattern does not supply the bound without its other hypotheses.

For b_n=1/n, absolute convergence fails by the harmonic argument. To see concretely why order matters, let H_m=Σ_(j=1 to m)1/j. The usual alternating sum through 2m terms is H_(2m)−H_m. Also H_(2m)−H_m=(1/m)Σ_(j=1 to m)1/(1+j/m), a right Riemann sum tending to ∫_1^2 dx/x=ln 2. The integral definition and logarithm are shared in [Construct a logarithm from accumulated reciprocal change](../../chemistry/core/Construct-a-logarithm-from-accumulated-reciprocal-change.lingua#r_048F416B6E95AE137A181635B7).

Now take two positive terms followed by one negative term, preserving the order within each sign: 1+1/3−1/2+1/5+1/7−1/4+…. After m complete triples, finite algebra gives H_(4m)−(H_(2m)+H_m)/2. Rewrite this as [H_(4m)−H_(2m)]+[H_(2m)−H_m]/2; the limit is (3/2)ln 2. The at-most-two terms of an incomplete triple tend to zero, so the full reordered sequence has the same new limit. Every original term appears exactly once, yet the sum changed. No unrestricted infinite cancellation was used in the proof.

Absolute convergence prevents this effect. Given ε>0, choose an original finite prefix leaving absolute tail below ε. Every sufficiently long partial sum of any permutation includes that whole prefix. The discrepancy from the original sum consists only of some included or omitted tail terms and has magnitude at most the full absolute tail. Hence the permuted partial sums have the same limit. The argument requires a genuine permutation: no omitted or infinitely repeated term.

**Work:** prove the alternating-tail estimate from the two bounding subsequences. Reconstruct the finite harmonic identities above and justify the Riemann-sum limit. Explain why the theorem does not license subtracting two divergent positive totals. Give a nonnegative absolutely summable account and an explicit change of indexing whose finite partitions exhaust all its terms; state why that change preserves the sum. Read [Basic Analysis I by Jiri Lebl](../sources/Basic-Analysis-I-by-Jiri-Lebl.lingua#r_00D018D5107F06EE77E9066592) sections 2.5–2.6 for further development; those full sections are assigned, not claimed wholly audited here.

**Continue:** [Ask whether one approximation bound covers the whole input domain](Ask-whether-one-approximation-bound-covers-the-whole-input-domain.lingua#r_000ADDED5D8664575C3F4E2B6B).

